  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "Fixed: a ¥1,500 invoice now produces a gateway amount of 1500, not 150000, and `npm test` passes (9 of 9).\n\n**Root cause:** `buildChargePayload` in `src/payments/gateway/payload.js` lower-cases the currency to `\"jpy\"` for the gateway. It then passes that lower-case code to `toMinor`. The currency registry only had upper-case keys, so `\"jpy\"` was not found and the lookup fell back to the default of 2 decimal places. JPY and KRW have none, so the amount was multiplied by 100. USD and EUR have 2 decimals, which is why they were fine.\n\n**Fix:** In `src/lib/currency/registry.js`, the lookups (`exponent`, `isSupported`, `symbol`) now trim and upper-case the code before searching. This fixes the charge path and also any other caller that passes a lower-case code, such as refunds, payouts, coupons and late fees.\n\n**Regression tests** in `test/charge.test.js`:\n- JPY 1500 and KRW 20000 charge in whole units.\n- KWD 1.5 charges 1500 (three decimal places).\n- `toMinor` gives the same result for `\"jpy\"` and `\"JPY\"`.\n\nI didn't touch `src/legacy/money.js`, which hardcodes ×100, because it's only used by the USD revenue export.\n\nIf any ¥/₩ customers were already charged at 100× before this fix, those charges will need to be refunded separately. This change doesn't cover that.",
